  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-5x-9y=-12 \\\\ \\frac{5}{6}x-2y=9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the system of equations given above.",
  "worked_solution": "To get rid of the fractions in the second equation, we must multiply the second equation by the lowest common denominator of the fractions, which is $6.$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-5x-9y=-12 \\\\ \\frac{5}{6}x-2y=9 & |\\,\\times 6\\end{aligned}\\,\u27f9\\,{\\begin{aligned}-5x-9y=-12 \\\\ 5x-12y=54\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that the $-5x$ term in the first equation can cancel with the $5x$ term in the second equation.\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $x$-variable, and allows us to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\cancel{5x}-9y & =-12 \\\\ \\cancel{5x}-12y & =54 \\\\ -21y & =42 \\\\ y & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $y=-2$ into the equation $-5x-9y=-12$ to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5x-9y & =-12 \\\\ -5x-9\\cdot (-2) & =-12 \\\\ -5x+18 & =-12 \\\\ -5x & =-30 \\\\ x & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is $x=6,\\,y=-2.$",
